\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSFileHandle is an object that represents an open file or a communications channel. It enables programs to read data from or write data to the represented file or channel. You can use other OpenStep methods for reading from and writing to files\'d0NSFileManager\'27s \b contentsAtPath:\b0  and NSData\'27s \b writeToFile:atomically:\b0  are but a couple of examples. Why would you use NSFileHandle then? What are its advantages?\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab NSFileHandle gives you greater control over input/output operations on files. It allows more manipulatve operations on and within open files, such as seeking, truncating. and reading and writing at an exact position within a file (the file pointer). Other OpenStep methods read or write a file in its entirety; with NSFileHandle, you can range over an open file and insert, extract, and delete data.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The scope of NSFileHandle is not limited to files. It provides the only OpenStep object that can read and write to communications channels such as those implemented by sockets, pipes, and devices. \
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSFileHandle makes possible asynchronous background communication. With it a program can connect to, and read from, a socket in a separate thread. (See Background Inter-Process Communication Using Sockets below for details on how this is done.)\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSFileHandle enhances application portability. Its API supports I/O operations on UNIX file systems as well as on certain non-UNIX file sysrems such as Windows NT. Because this API  is closer in semantics to traditional UNIX file I/O,  it makes it easier for UNIX programmers to port applications.\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 Instances of NSPipe, a class closely related to NSFileHandle, represent pipes: unidirectional interprocess communication channels that are found on both UNIX systems and on Windows NT. See the NSPipe specification for details.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 As a convenience, NSFileHandle provides class methods that create objects representing files and devices in the file system and that return objects representing the standard input, standard output, and standard error devices. You can also create NSFileHandles from UNIX file descriptors or non-UNIX file handles (particularly Windows HANDLEs) using the \b initWithFileDescriptor:\b0  and \b initWithNativeHandle:\b0  methods. If you create NSFileHandles with these last two methods, you "own" the represented descriptor or handle and are responsible for removing it from system tables, usually by sending the object a \b closeFile\b0  message.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Background Inter-Process Communication Using Sockets\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Sockets are full-duplex communication channels between processes either local to the same host machine or where one process is on a remote host. Unlike pipes, in which data goes in one direction only, sockets allow processes both to send and receive data. NSFileHandle facilitates communciation over stream-type sockets by providing mechanisms run in background threads that accept socket connections and read from sockets.\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSFileHandle currently handles only communciation through stream-type sockets. If you want to use datagrams or other types of sockets, you must create and manage the connection using native system routines.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The process on one end of the communication channel (the server) starts by creating and preparing a socket using system routines. These routines vary slightly between UNIX and non-UNIX systems (such as Windows), but consist of the same sequence of steps:\
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s55 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 1.\tab Create a stream-type socket of a certain protocol.\
\fs16\
\s56 \fs28 2.\tab Bind a name to the socket.\
\fs16\
\fs28 3.\tab Listen for incoming connections to the socket.\
\fs16\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Typically the other process (the client) then locates the named socket created by the first process. Instead of accepting a connection to the socket by calling the appropriate system routine, the client performs the following sequence of steps:\
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s55 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 1.\tab It creates an NSFileHandle using the socket identifier as argument to \b initWithFileDescriptor:\b0  or \b initWithNativeHandle:\b0 , whichever is appropriate to the system on which the socket was created. \
\fs16\
\s56 \fs28 2.\tab It adds itself as an observer of NSFileHandleConnectionAcceptedNotification.\
\fs16\
\fs28 3.\tab It sends \b acceptConnectionInBackgroundAndNotify\b0  to this NSFileHandle. This method accepts the connection in the background, creates a new NSFileHandle from the new socket descriptor, and posts a NSFileHandleConnectionAcceptedNotification. \
\fs16\
\fs28 4.\tab In a method implemented to respond to this notification, the client extracts the NSFileHandle representing the "near" socket of the connection from the notification\'27s \b userInfo\b0  dictionary; it uses the NSFileHandleNotificationFileHandleItem key to do this.\
\fs16\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 The client can now send data to the other process over the communications channel by sending \b writeData:\b0  to the NSFileHandle. (Note that \b writeData:\b0  can block.) The client can also read data directly from the NSFileHandle, but this would cause the process to block until the socket connection was closed, so it is usually better to read in the background. To do this, the process must:\
\fs14\
\fs2\
\fs28 \pard \s56 \li2340 \fi-376 \ri1007 \ql \tx2340 \tx3981 \tx4359 5.\tab Add itself as an observer of NSFileHandleReadCompletionNotification or NSFileHandleReadToEndOfFileCompletionNotification.\
\fs16\
\fs28 6.\tab Send \b readInBackgroundAndNotify\b0  or \b readToEndOfFileInBackgroundAndNotify\b0  to this NSFileHandle. The former method sends a notification after each transmission of data; the latter method accumulates data and sends a notification only after the sending process shuts down its end of the connection.\
\fs16\
\fs28 7.\tab In a method implemented to respond to either of these notifications, the process extracts the transmitted or accumulated data from the notification\'27s \b userInfo\b0  dictionary by using the NSFileHandleNotificationDataItem key.\
\fs16\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 You close the communications channel in both directions by sending \b closeFile\b0  to the NSFileHandle; either process can partially or totally close communication across the socket connection with a system-specific shutdown command.\
\fs14\
\fs56\
